#use(std/code)
#use(std/control)

#section{Control Flow}::

#code{#use(std/control)} adds two calls whose #emph{indented block} is a lazy
body — loops and conditionals without hand-writing a #code{#(...)} escape. Both
are thin wrappers over host code (the language is always available); they exist
for the common, readable cases

#section{##foreach — Loops}
	#code|{#foreach(seq 'x)}| iterates any sequence (a list, #code{#(in-range …)},
	a stream, a generator …). Each pass forks the context and binds #code{x} to
	the element; the block is the body — read the element with
	#code{#(context-ref 'x)}. The per-pass contents splice in document order:

	#code(lang: "plain")
		#list(style: "1")
			#foreach((in-range 1 4) 'n)
				#item{Row #(context-ref 'n)}

	Live:

	#list(style: "1")
		#foreach((in-range 1 4) 'n)
			#item{Row #(context-ref 'n)}

#section{##showif — Conditionals}
	#code{#showif(test)} forces and emits its block when #code{test} is truthy,
	and nothing otherwise — an #code{if} with no #code{else}. Because the body is
	lazy, a dropped branch is never evaluated:

	#code(lang: "plain")
		#showif(#t){Shown.} #showif(#f){Never evaluated.}

	Live:

	#showif(#t){Shown.} #showif(#f){Never evaluated.}

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		The names are #code{foreach} / #code{showif}, not #code{for} / #code{if} / #code{when}: a plugin's bindings share
		the document namespace with host code, so shadowing Racket's control forms would break #code{#(for …)} /
		#code{#(if …)} inside escapes
	#item
		The loop variable is a #strong{context key} (a quoted symbol), read with #code{#(context-ref 'x)}. It lives in a
		#emph{forked} context, so it never leaks past the loop and a nested #code{#foreach} keeps its own binding
	#item
		The body is forced once per element, so its side effects — a #code{#section}, a #code{#def} — run per iteration:
		that is how a loop generates many numbered sections or definitions
	#item
		Both take inline, #code{: rest-of-line} and indented-block bodies alike; #code|{#foreach(seq 'x){…}}| and the
		block form are equivalent
	#item
		For anything past a loop or a one-armed conditional — an #code{else}, an accumulator, early exit — drop to host
		code: #code{#(cond …)}, #code{#(for/list …)}, #code{#(when …)}
